      intro: 'A screen is rarely one component. You compose it from child components, each with its own class, template, and state. The parent writes the child tag where the piece belongs, and each place the tag appears is a separate instance with separate state. Picture a garden: the garden is the parent, and each flower in it is a child. Lessons 8 to 11 all live in this garden. The demo plants the same flower three times. Tap Bloom inside one flower: only that flower counts its blooms. The other two do not move, because each instance keeps its own fields.',
      diagram: '+-------------------------------+\n| Garden (the parent)           |\n|                               |\n|  +---------+  +---------+     |\n|  | Flower  |  | Flower  |     |\n|  | blooms 0|  | blooms 0|     |\n|  +---------+  +---------+     |\n|                               |\n|  +---------+                  |\n|  | Flower  |                  |\n|  | blooms 0|                  |\n|  +---------+                  |\n+-------------------------------+\n\nEach flower keeps its own count.\nTap Bloom in one flower: only\nthat flower changes.',
